<component type="desktop-application" xmlns="https://specifications.freedesktop.org/metainfo/1.0">
  <id>@app-id@</id>
  <launchable type="desktop-id">@app-id@.desktop</launchable>
  <name>Commune</name>
  <summary>Chat on Matrix</summary>
  <description>
    <p>
      Commune is a Matrix messaging app written in Rust. Its interface is optimized for
      collaboration in large groups, such as free software projects, and will fit all screens, big or small.
    </p>
    <p>Highlights:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Find rooms to discuss your favorite topics, or talk privately to people, securely thanks to end-to-end encryption</li>
      <li>Send rich formatted messages, files, or your current location</li>
      <li>Reply to specific messages, react with emoji, mention users or rooms, edit or remove messages</li>
      <li>View images, and play audio and video directly in the conversation</li>
      <li>Send and manage custom sticker and emoticon packs</li>
      <li>See who has read messages, and who is typing</li>
      <li>Log into multiple accounts at once (with Single-Sign On support)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Commune is a fork of Fractal, the Matrix client for GNOME, and is not affiliated with or
      endorsed by the Fractal project or the GNOME project.
    </p>
  </description>
  <branding>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="light">#f9d5d7</color>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="dark">#a2131a</color>
  </branding>
  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>GPL-3.0+</project_license>
  <developer id="io.github.steeb_k">
    <name>steeb-k</name>
  </developer>
  <translation type="gettext">commune</translation>

  <screenshots>
    <screenshot type="default">
      <image type="source">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/steeb-k/commune/main/screenshots/main.png</image>
      <caption>Commune’s main window</caption>
    </screenshot>
    <screenshot>
      <image type="source">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/steeb-k/commune/main/screenshots/media-history.png</image>
      <caption>View the media history of a Matrix room</caption>
    </screenshot>
    <screenshot>
      <image type="source">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/steeb-k/commune/main/screenshots/adaptive.png</image>
      <caption>Commune’s interface adapts to small screens</caption>
    </screenshot>
  </screenshots>

  <url type="homepage">https://github.com/steeb-k/commune</url>
  <url type="vcs-browser">https://github.com/steeb-k/commune</url>
  <url type="bugtracker">https://github.com/steeb-k/commune/issues</url>
  <url type="contribute">https://github.com/steeb-k/commune/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md</url>

  <requires>
    <display_length compare="ge">360</display_length>
    <internet>always</internet>
  </requires>
  <supports>
    <control>keyboard</control>
    <control>pointing</control>
    <control>touch</control>
  </supports>

  <content_rating type="oars-1.1">
    <content_attribute id="social-chat">intense</content_attribute>
    <content_attribute id="social-audio">intense</content_attribute>
  </content_rating>

  <releases>@development-release@
    <release version="1~rc4" type="development" date="2026-09-13">
      <description>
        <p>
          The fourth release candidate is the first whose updater has been watched working on
          every platform it runs on. On Windows, an installation of an earlier candidate cannot
          update itself to this one: install it from the release page once, and it updates itself
          from then on.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            On Windows, pressing Update downloaded the package and then nothing happened: the
            helper that runs the installer was started without a console and waited forever. It
            runs the installer now, and gives up waiting after two minutes rather than never.
          </li>
          <li>
            On Windows, the package installs every one of its files. Windows Installer would
            keep a library that carried a higher version than the one arriving, then remove it
            with the old version, and report success over an application that could not start.
          </li>
          <li>
            On Android, checking for updates failed with a sentence about a missing runtime. The
            check and the download run where they should, and the rows say what actually went
            wrong, in words.
          </li>
          <li>
            A candidate follows the release-candidate channel by default. The first three followed
            the stable channel, where nothing has been published yet, and reported that as an
            unreachable server; a channel with nothing on it now says so.
          </li>
          <li>
            The Version row says which version and which build is running, on both platforms.
          </li>
          <li>
            On Android, a release can be skipped, the automatic check runs when the app starts
            rather than only when Settings is open and says what it found, and an installer that
            is cancelled hands the Update button back.
          </li>
          <li>
            Opening a busy room no longer closes Commune a second time: a member the room list
            was still placing could be dropped while a read receipt pointed at it.
          </li>
          <li>
            The composer is hidden while the thread list or the pinned messages take the place of
            the timeline, and a picture in the media history that cannot be loaded, or is not
            shown because previews are off, says so with an icon instead of a dark tile.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="1~rc3" type="development" date="2026-09-13">
      <description>
        <p>
          The third release candidate fixes the fault that closed the first two the moment a room
          with more than two people was opened.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Opening a room with more than two members no longer closes Commune on the spot. The
            list of members let go of a member while it was still being counted, and every desktop
            build of the first two candidates carried that.
          </li>
          <li>
            Every other list Commune keeps, of rooms, accounts, reactions, media, sessions and
            verifications, was checked for the same fault and hardened the same way.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="1~rc2" type="development" date="2026-09-12">
      <description>
        <p>
          The second release candidate answers what the first one got wrong on a Mac, and one
          thing it got wrong everywhere.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A file can be dropped onto the message bar as well as onto the messages, on every
            platform.
          </li>
          <li>
            On macOS, the media viewer no longer draws a second set of window buttons over the
            picture, and its back button sits to the right of the real ones.
          </li>
          <li>
            On macOS, leaving a fullscreened picture no longer leaves parts of the window showing
            whatever was behind it.
          </li>
          <li>
            On macOS, closing the window puts it away and leaves Commune running in the Dock,
            where it keeps receiving messages and posting notifications. Command-W and Command-Q
            both close the window; Quit, from the menu or the Dock, is what ends it. Clicking the
            Dock icon brings the window back.
          </li>
          <li>
            On macOS, a run no longer logs a theme parser error for every message body.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="1~rc1" type="development" date="2026-09-12">
      <description>
        <p>
          Commune is a fork of Fractal 14.1 with its own name, icon and settings, so it installs
          and runs alongside Fractal without either app disturbing the other’s sessions or
          preferences. This first release candidate is everything that has grown since the fork,
          on top of everything Fractal already did.
        </p>
        <p>Conversations got the most attention:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Voice and video calls between two people. A call opens in a window of its own, rings
            on the other side, and leaves a row in the timeline where it happened. Group calls are
            not supported yet.
          </li>
          <li>
            Threads. A message that started one says so, a thread can be read and replied to on a
            page of its own, and all the threads of a room are listed together.
          </li>
          <li>
            Searching a room’s messages, with results opening a timeline centred on the match.
            Encrypted rooms are searched through an index built from the messages the room has
            already loaded.
          </li>
          <li>Voice messages can now be recorded, not only played back.</li>
          <li>
            Several files can be attached at once, from the file dialog, a drop, or the share
            sheet. Each becomes its own message, sent in the order they were picked.
          </li>
          <li>
            Custom sticker and emoticon packs can be browsed, added, edited and removed from
            account settings and from room details, and used from the composer. Custom emoticons
            render inline, complete from their shortcodes, and a message made only of them is
            shown larger.
          </li>
          <li>A GIF picker in the composer.</li>
          <li>Link previews under messages, with a switch to turn them off.</li>
          <li>Quick reactions are drawn from the emoji recently used on any client of the account.</li>
          <li>Pinning messages, and a page that shows what a room has pinned.</li>
          <li>
            Every message in an encrypted room carries a shield that says how far its sender can
            be trusted, and a message that could not be decrypted now says why.
          </li>
          <li>A message that failed to send can be sent again, rather than only discarded.</li>
          <li>
            A Chat Bubbles switch draws the timeline with your own messages on the right, and a
            Dark Mode switch keeps the app dark whatever the system says.
          </li>
          <li>
            The media history groups a room’s pictures and videos by age, shows the date as you
            scroll past it, and opens on the most recent.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Rooms and spaces:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A Spaces section in the sidebar. Opening a space lists the rooms and subspaces inside
            it, and a new room can be put in a space or restricted to its members.
          </li>
          <li>
            Previewing a world-readable room before joining it, from Explore and from a space’s
            room list.
          </li>
          <li>
            Presence: who is around, shown in the member list, on profiles and on direct chats,
            with a switch for whether you share your own.
          </li>
          <li>A person’s profile lists the rooms you share with them.</li>
          <li>Favourites keep the order another client gave them.</li>
          <li>The knock banner remembers which requests you have already looked at.</li>
          <li>Server notices from the homeserver get their own sidebar section and a banner.</li>
          <li>
            Moderation rules in a policy room read as sentences: who recommended banning what, and
            why. So do a room’s policy server and server ACL changes.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Accounts and moderation:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Creating an account, and resetting a forgotten password, without leaving the app. The
            greeter offers matrix.org instead of asking for a homeserver first.
          </li>
          <li>
            The email addresses and phone numbers on an account are listed in settings, where an
            email address can be added or removed.
          </li>
          <li>
            The predefined notification rules, for mentions of you or of the whole room, invites
            and incoming calls, can be switched on and off.
          </li>
          <li>
            An identity server can be chosen, or declined, and with one set somebody can be invited
            to a room by email address.
          </li>
          <li>
            Editing a restricted room’s join rule, showing and editing which servers can take part
            in a room, and reporting rooms and users.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Beyond Linux:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Commune builds and runs on macOS and Windows, shipped as a disk image and an installer.
            Sessions are kept in the Keychain and the Credential Manager, notifications arrive
            through the system, and matrix: links open the app.
          </li>
          <li>
            Commune notices when a new release exists, and on Windows, macOS and Android it can
            download and install it itself. A Flatpak installation updates through the remote it
            came from instead. There is a switch in settings for whether to check at all.
          </li>
          <li>
            A separate Android client, built on the same core as the desktop app. It covers the
            everyday: conversations and threads, media, stickers and GIFs, calls, verification by
            emoji or QR code, several accounts, and notifications over UnifiedPush or background
            sync.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Everything else is Fractal 14.1, whose release notes remain the best description of what
          Commune inherits. As usual, this release includes other improvements and fixes thanks to
          our upstream projects, and above all to the Fractal contributors whose work all of this
          is built on.
        </p>
        <p>
          As the version implies, this candidate is meant to be installed and used. That is how a
          release candidate earns the plain release that follows it, and what it gets wrong is what
          1.rc2 is for.
        </p>
      </description>
    </release>
  </releases>
</component>
